[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Logic Blocks in Svelte: if, each, and await"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte provides powerful built-in control flow structures directly inside your templates. Unlike traditional frameworks that rely on JavaScript array methods or ternary operators inside JSX, Svelte uses clean, readable logic blocks like {#if}, {#each}, and {#await}."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Rendering with {#if}"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The {#if} block allows you to conditionally render markup based on JavaScript expressions. You can also chain alternative conditions using {:else if} and provide a fallback using {:else}."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"{#if user.loggedIn}
  <p>Welcome back, {user.name}!</p>
{:else if user.isTrial}
  <p>Your trial period is active.</p>
{:else}
  <p>Please log in to continue.</p>
{/if}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Iterating Over Lists with {#each}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To render lists of items, Svelte uses the {#each} block. For optimal performance and state preservation during DOM updates, you should provide a unique key identifier for each item."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<ul>
  {#each items as item, index (item.id)}
    <li>{index + 1}: {item.text}</li>
  {:else}
    <li>No items found in the list.</li>
  {/each}
</ul>"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Asynchronous Data with {#await}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Handling Promises directly in the UI can often be verbose. Svelte simplifies asynchronous operations using the {#await} block, automatically managing pending, fulfilled, and rejected states."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"{#await promise}
  <p>Loading data...</p>
{:then data}
  <p>The value is: {data}</p>
{:catch error}
  <p style="color: red;">An error occurred: {error.message}</p>
{/await}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use {#if} blocks for straightforward conditional rendering without complex ternary nesting."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use keyed each blocks (e.g., {#each items as item (item.id)}) when dealing with mutable lists."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use {#await} blocks to gracefully handle network requests and promise resolutions directly in your templates."}]
